WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.965
Scott Young is the ultimate adventurer in learning.

2
00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:12.510
He's compressed the entire four-year MIT curriculum for computer science into one year

3
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.090
of independent learning and is now wrapping up

4
00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.385
a year's travel learning four different languages,

5
00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:20.935
Spanish, Portuguese, Chinese, and

6
00:00:20.935 --> 00:00:24.655
Korean through total immersion in each of the countries.

7
00:00:24.655 --> 00:00:28.655
Scott doesn't just study learning from an academic perspective.

8
00:00:28.655 --> 00:00:33.450
He immerses himself in learning at perceptively observes the results,

9
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.320
so that we can all gain from it.

10
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.300
So, it's a pleasure to welcome Scott Young.

11
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.710
Scott, I'm always excited to follow your adventures.

12
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.445
So tell me, where are you now and what is your latest learning challenge?

13
00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:48.230
Right. So, right now at the moment,

14
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.700
I'm actually in South Korea in Seoul,

15
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.945
not too far away from Gangnam actually,

16
00:00:52.945 --> 00:00:54.920
and I'm learning Korean,

17
00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.010
and this is part of a larger project where a friend and I are traveling for a year,

18
00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:03.775
staying three months in four different countries, so three months each,

19
00:01:03.775 --> 00:01:06.290
trying to learn the language of

20
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:10.150
that country through not speaking English as much as possible.

21
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:16.215
Wow, I'm just so impressed just sort of a Marco Polo of learning.

22
00:01:16.215 --> 00:01:20.840
I was also impressed by your MIT challenge in learning.

23
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.400
What you did was you compressed a four-year curriculum

24
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.440
of computer science into one year of independent learning.

25
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.675
There are a lot of questions I have for you in relation to

26
00:01:31.675 --> 00:01:35.845
that experience as well as your language learning experience.

27
00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:38.595
So first, can you tell us,

28
00:01:38.595 --> 00:01:42.490
how do you avoid illusions of competence in learning?

29
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.910
Right. Well, I'm a big fan of trying to dive

30
00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:49.480
into a position where you might be wrong as soon as possible.

31
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:50.900
So when you're just listening to a lecture,

32
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.250
reading a textbook, you can't ever really be wrong.

33
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.960
There's no real check on whether you know what you know and I really like

34
00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.840
getting into problems as quickly as possible, with a language,

35
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.050
trying to speak as quickly as possible,

36
00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.700
or if not speaking as quickly as possible,

37
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.025
testing my listening comprehension in a strict way.

38
00:02:10.025 --> 00:02:11.695
So with the MIT challenge,

39
00:02:11.695 --> 00:02:13.640
I would work on problem sets,

40
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:17.330
and I will do my best to do the problem sets without having

41
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:21.710
the solutions at hand and try my best on any problem,

42
00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.345
and if I got stuck, if I couldn't finish, then of course,

43
00:02:24.345 --> 00:02:27.060
go to the problem and check to see the solution and learn it.

44
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.680
But I think, only if you feel that grind of it first,

45
00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.785
if you feel that tension,

46
00:02:30.785 --> 00:02:33.640
that stress that you're not quite sure how to finish it,

47
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.430
only then will you really remember how to do the problem for the future.

48
00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.330
So if you don't have that feeling of not being sure how to do it and that strain,

49
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.340
I think it's hard to really improve your knowledge.

50
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.080
That goes right along with

51
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.820
our own learning philosophy in this course which

52
00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.800
is test yourself as frequently as possible.

53
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.660
So, one of the approaches that you

54
00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.110
have to learning that I absolutely love is that of self explanation.

55
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:04.720
Can you explain to our viewers a little bit what you mean by self explanation?

56
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.600
What is it and what kind of benefits can you get from it?

57
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.590
Right. Well, I got this idea of reading a biography of Richard Feynman,

58
00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.540
he was a Nobel Prize winning physicist,

59
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.700
and I forget the exact method that he used himself,

60
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.830
but I remember him talking about being a Nobel Prize winner in theoretical Physics,

61
00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.910
he's a smart guy, and he was talking about a particular academic paper,

62
00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.930
particular concept he didn't understand,

63
00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.560
and his approach wasn't to throw his hands up and say,

64
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.690
"Well, I don't get it, it hurts,

65
00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:36.110
too difficult", but he went through meticulously not only trying to

66
00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:40.130
understand everything that was in that paper but of the papers it sourced.

67
00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.870
He read through them very carefully and combed through them and

68
00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.855
tried to make sure he understood all the supporting ideas.

69
00:03:45.855 --> 00:03:49.370
So for me, I've kind of adapted that into this idea of taking

70
00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.150
a blank piece of paper out and writing as if I'm trying

71
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:57.020
to teach someone else what this idea is all about or what this process

72
00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.270
for solving a particular type of problem is all about or what it means.

73
00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:05.545
What I find happens is that you usually get to points where you have some friction,

74
00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.090
where you have to be too vague or you can't

75
00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:11.750
really be as exact and precise as you want to be,

76
00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.080
and those are usually the things that you don't understand.

77
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.460
So, you can go back to your notes,

78
00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:16.855
you can go back to the textbook,

79
00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:20.515
look up that exact spot and figure out,

80
00:04:20.515 --> 00:04:22.000
"Oh, this is the part I'm missing.

81
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.830
I'm missing step three of this process.

82
00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.090
Or I don't really understand why step three works,

83
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.300
maybe I can ask someone,

84
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.540
a teacher or a friend."

85
00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.430
I think it's so smart to go look and intuit how

86
00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.920
other very creative people have

87
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.165
approached their problem-solving and I always love Feynman's approach.

88
00:04:41.165 --> 00:04:49.870
He always said that it's very important that the first rule is not to fool yourself,

89
00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.815
but you are the easiest person to fool.

90
00:04:52.815 --> 00:04:58.550
So, I just love how you're learning from other learners.

91
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:07.180
One of your approaches that I really like is that of creating vivid examples.

92
00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.525
Can you give us an example of that?

93
00:05:09.525 --> 00:05:13.290
So, the mind doesn't learn abstract things very well.

94
00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.980
It works a lot better when you have something very concrete you can point to and say,

95
00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.520
"Oh, this is how it works."

96
00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:24.095
Math and science is often full of things that are just pure abstraction.

97
00:05:24.095 --> 00:05:27.445
They only connect very loosely to things we can touch and feel.

98
00:05:27.445 --> 00:05:31.850
So, what I try to do is find simple analogies or metaphors

99
00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:36.790
and try to test them or see how to make them fit with the thing I'm trying to learn.

100
00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:41.990
So, it's kind of like looking through your mind for examples or

101
00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.900
stories or things that you are familiar with and

102
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.020
like fitting a jigsaw piece into a puzzle,

103
00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.610
trying to figure out what's the right piece.

104
00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.290
So, I'll give you a quick example of that.

105
00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:53.285
I was learning about electricity,

106
00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:56.435
and one of the concepts you learn early on is voltage.

107
00:05:56.435 --> 00:05:59.570
Now, I didn't have a good intuition of what voltage was.

108
00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.360
So, I'm trying to make a mental picture of

109
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.710
what voltage can be and I'm thinking about an electrical circuit.

110
00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:08.535
Well, it's a little bit like pipes with water.

111
00:06:08.535 --> 00:06:12.115
So, if the electrical circuit's like pipes with water,

112
00:06:12.115 --> 00:06:13.875
then what are all the components?

113
00:06:13.875 --> 00:06:17.910
Current's pretty easy, that's like the water flowing, I can get that.

114
00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.180
But what was voltage?

115
00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.215
I was like, was it like pressure?

116
00:06:21.215 --> 00:06:23.240
But if you look at the equations for

117
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.220
pressure and if you look at the equations for voltage,

118
00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.795
they don't really look alike.

119
00:06:26.795 --> 00:06:29.170
So, that felt wrong.

120
00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:32.235
I realized that if you've studied electricity,

121
00:06:32.235 --> 00:06:35.510
you've usually also studied the gravity

122
00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:40.610
beforehand and what was the same was electric potential and gravitational potential.

123
00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.785
So, oh, voltage is like height.

124
00:06:42.785 --> 00:06:44.480
So I can imagine in my head that

125
00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:50.690
the high voltage wires are like pipes that are physically higher than the other pipes.

126
00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:52.810
So the water, when it rushes down,

127
00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.250
when it goes down from the high level to low level,

128
00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:59.330
it gives off a lot more energy because it's falling and that metaphor really

129
00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:03.790
helped me because not only was it something concrete that I'll never forget,

130
00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.950
but it also was fairly accurate and that I

131
00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.845
tested out a couple of things like pressure and I found,

132
00:07:08.845 --> 00:07:10.290
okay, this is the one that works.

133
00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.120
Sometimes, this process can take a little bit of time.

134
00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.820
I'm recapping this in two seconds but it really took me

135
00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:19.165
about an hour to to comb through the notes to figure this one out.

136
00:07:19.165 --> 00:07:21.510
But you can ask professors,

137
00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:22.930
you can ask, "Hey,

138
00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:24.370
is this kind of like this?"

139
00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:27.850
Someone who really understands it can help you out or you can just do what I

140
00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:31.240
did and just try to fit different puzzle pieces and see what fits,

141
00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.070
because even if you don't find a good puzzle piece,

142
00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.315
you can still learn a lot more that way.

143
00:07:35.315 --> 00:07:37.520
Oh, I couldn't agree more.

144
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.435
I do find that as the years have gone by,

145
00:07:40.435 --> 00:07:44.310
the most creative professors who I've worked with,

146
00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:48.520
researchers are always the ones who have used these kinds of analogies,

147
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:54.160
and the ones who are more pedantic a little bit more by the book.

148
00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.800
Often artists creative about how they approach things,

149
00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:02.125
so I think that's a very intelligent way of going at everything.

150
00:08:02.125 --> 00:08:06.575
Can you talk to our viewers just a little bit about motivation.

151
00:08:06.575 --> 00:08:10.255
How can you develop a passion for learning perhaps

152
00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:14.100
even in subjects you think you don't have a passion for?

153
00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.830
Any suggestions for mental tools people

154
00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.545
can use to help motivate themselves in their learning?

155
00:08:19.545 --> 00:08:23.675
Well, I love this question because right now I'm learning languages

156
00:08:23.675 --> 00:08:28.900
and I'm currently learning my sixth Language, Korean right now.

157
00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:32.075
We're doing it in very tight time constraints so I've gotten

158
00:08:32.075 --> 00:08:35.310
a few e-mails from people who have heard about the project and they said,

159
00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:37.495
"Well, maybe you and your friend who are doing this have

160
00:08:37.495 --> 00:08:42.160
this natural talent for languages, this genetic gift."

161
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.220
I think it's so funny because I remember

162
00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.530
the first time I was trying to learn a foreign language, French,

163
00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:48.500
we spent about the same amount of time that I'm spending

164
00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.550
here and I barely passed the exam,

165
00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.920
I got a D. I feel like I'm a fairly smart fellow on other things.

166
00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.500
So I didn't really let it get to me,

167
00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.790
but I think it's true for a lot of subjects that being

168
00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:02.955
intelligent within that subject is

169
00:09:02.955 --> 00:09:05.850
often a factor of just how much exposure you've had to it.

170
00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.025
So, if you're not used to Math,

171
00:09:08.025 --> 00:09:11.215
don't take that as a sign that maybe you're bad at Math but just that you need to

172
00:09:11.215 --> 00:09:14.415
put more time in and you'll get more motivation.

173
00:09:14.415 --> 00:09:16.355
It'll be more easy to motivate yourself,

174
00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:18.600
you have more interest once you're better at it.

175
00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.790
You can get better at it by encouraging

176
00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:24.970
yourself to take on little steps, little mini projects.

177
00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:26.530
Once you complete that project,

178
00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:27.670
you build more confidence.

179
00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.960
With more confidence, you can do more things,

180
00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.730
you can understand more things and it becomes more interesting.

181
00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.650
I loved your story that you gave of learning Math because I think that's

182
00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.960
just a perfect example and it's too bad that

183
00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:41.665
a lot of people just don't conceptualize the world that way.

184
00:09:41.665 --> 00:09:44.870
One thing that I really like about your own story is,

185
00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:48.555
you failed but you just learnt from failure.

186
00:09:48.555 --> 00:09:50.375
You didn't allow that to get to you.

187
00:09:50.375 --> 00:09:54.960
You just shifted your strategies and figured out what strategy was more successful.

188
00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:02.795
I think that's a sign of the best learners is not to be set back by failure,

189
00:10:02.795 --> 00:10:04.175
they just learn from it.

190
00:10:04.175 --> 00:10:05.755
Absolutely.

191
00:10:05.755 --> 00:10:11.195
So, I love how you've developed projects for self-education.

192
00:10:11.195 --> 00:10:14.615
Can you tell us a little more about what you've done this way and how

193
00:10:14.615 --> 00:10:19.400
our viewers might go about developing their own projects for self-education.

194
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.660
Right. So, I actually got this idea from a good friend.

195
00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.720
He's also and I think your recommended resources Benny Lewis.

196
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.390
He was someone who told me,

197
00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:31.020
I remember he told me in person one day it's always have a mission.

198
00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:36.920
Basically just this idea of he would pick these three month missions for learning

199
00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.705
languages because that was usually how long he could secure

200
00:10:39.705 --> 00:10:43.580
a tourist visa to this place to try to learn as much as possible in three months.

201
00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.860
But it really struck me how different that was from the regular approach,

202
00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.490
the regular approach of going and I'm going to just

203
00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:52.940
learn this and I don't really have

204
00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:56.880
any concrete goals and I don't really have any specific motivation,

205
00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.340
and of course it tapers off and you don't achieve that much.

206
00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:05.225
What I found really helpful is making very concrete projects that are exciting to me.

207
00:11:05.225 --> 00:11:06.380
Something that you know,

208
00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:07.550
this is really interesting.

209
00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.515
So, when I did the MIT challenge,

210
00:11:09.515 --> 00:11:11.930
just this idea obsessed me.

211
00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.820
This idea of you know would it be possible to learn the things that

212
00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:18.360
an MIT student would learn in school without going to MIT?

213
00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.460
Where there's language learning project you know,

214
00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:24.840
would it be possible to get to a conversational level or a decent amount

215
00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:29.255
of level through complete immersion in four different countries in a year?

216
00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:34.020
So, I tend to pick these grand projects but I think you could pick something very simply.

217
00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.075
You could just say,

218
00:11:35.075 --> 00:11:37.295
"I want to try learning this over a month and

219
00:11:37.295 --> 00:11:39.800
I'm going to obsess about it and make it interesting."

220
00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:43.235
That's often how you can turn something that might otherwise be dull into

221
00:11:43.235 --> 00:11:47.010
something that fascinates you because it's this specific concrete challenge.

222
00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:48.790
I like this approach.

223
00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:53.450
It seems very similar to what I did with helping to create this MOOC.

224
00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:59.405
So, any tips on effective use of online resources?

225
00:11:59.405 --> 00:12:02.640
Right. So, this is I think particularly relevant to

226
00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.165
your audience because a MOOC audience you're

227
00:12:06.165 --> 00:12:09.260
signed up through this through Coursera and you probably know about

228
00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:12.485
Coursera and edX and all of the great MOOC platforms.

229
00:12:12.485 --> 00:12:14.030
I really think these platforms are

230
00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:17.780
the future because they have such high quality courses,

231
00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:21.720
but it's still early days and I think that there's still

232
00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.765
a lot of subjects that people would like to learn but maybe there isn't a MOOC for it.

233
00:12:25.765 --> 00:12:31.020
I think there's also some disadvantages to MOOCs in particular,

234
00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.750
if you want to learn something a little bit more advanced,

235
00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:37.490
MOOCs can sometimes be a bit harder because they tend to be

236
00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:41.860
written for audience with no prerequisites, no requirements.

237
00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.910
So, you might feel if you wanted to do some Physics,

238
00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:46.845
it isn't just an intro Physics class,

239
00:12:46.845 --> 00:12:50.100
this is a little bit harder because you know they are expecting that

240
00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.600
I haven't learned Calculus or I haven't learned something different.

241
00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:58.285
So, what I recommend is using MIT's OpenCourseWare. It's incredible.

242
00:12:58.285 --> 00:13:00.325
It has literally hundreds,

243
00:13:00.325 --> 00:13:02.575
I don't know whether it has thousands of courses,

244
